var tafsirData_14 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with three disconnected letters, as several chapters do, and the honest position held by many early scholars is that their complete meaning rests with Allah.\n\n\"˹This is˺ a Book which We have revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ so that you may lead people out of darkness and into light\" states the purpose of the whole revelation in a single image, and the image repays attention.\n\nNotice that darkness is mentioned in the plural in the Arabic and light in the singular. Commentators have observed this consistently across the Quran, and the point is significant. There are many kinds of darkness: disbelief, ignorance, superstition, injustice, moral confusion, and despair. There is one light.\n\nThat distinction matches experience. Ways of being wrong are endless and endlessly varied, while what is correct tends to be singular. A person can go astray in a thousand directions and arrive at only one truth.\n\nThe verb used describes leading people out, which suggests that human beings begin in darkness and must be brought out of it. Guidance is a rescue rather than a decoration.\n\n\"By the Will of their Lord\" places the necessary qualification. The Prophet leads, and the movement happens by Allah's permission. This preserves what the preceding chapters established repeatedly: the messenger conveys, and hearts are opened by Allah.\n\n\"To the Path of the Almighty, the Praiseworthy\" names the destination with two attributes.\n\nThe Arabic \"al-Aziz\" indicates might that cannot be overcome, honour that cannot be diminished. \"Al-Hamid\" indicates the One who is praised and deserving of all praise.\n\nThe pairing is deliberate and it answers something in human nature. Power without goodness produces fear rather than love. Goodness without power produces sympathy rather than trust. The path being described leads to One who has both, which is why the response He calls for combines awe with love.",
  "2": "\"Allah, to Whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\" completes the description of the One named at the end of the previous verse, and it does so by stating His ownership of everything.\n\nThe two attributes just mentioned, might and praiseworthiness, are now grounded in something concrete. He is not described as mighty in the abstract; everything in existence belongs to Him.\n\nThis ownership is total and it has practical consequences. Nothing a person possesses was ever independently his: not his wealth, not his body, not his time. The Quran develops this in many places, and it underlies the entire Islamic approach to charity, since giving is described as spending from what Allah has provided rather than surrendering something of one's own.\n\nIt also settles the question of authority. The One who owns everything has the right to command, and no created thing shares that right. The preceding chapters spent considerable effort on exactly this point, asking what the associated partners had created and finding no answer.\n\n\"And woe to the disbelievers because of a severe torment!\" turns sharply, and the shift in tone is deliberate.\n\nThe Arabic expression indicates destruction, ruin, and a cry of grief. It appears in the Quran as a warning of the worst outcome.\n\nPlacing this immediately after a statement of Allah's ownership is not arbitrary. If everything belongs to Him, then rejecting Him is not a neutral philosophical position. It is a creature denying the One who owns and sustains him while continuing to live on what He provides.\n\nThe severity of the warning at the opening of a chapter is characteristic of the Quran's method. Danger is named early and plainly, and the invitation follows. A doctor who conceals a diagnosis to avoid distressing a patient has not been kind.\n\nWhat follows in the next verse identifies exactly who these people are, and the description turns out to concern behaviour rather than mere opinion.",
  "3": "\"˹They are˺ the ones who favour the life of this world over the Hereafter\" identifies the disbelievers described in the previous verse, and it begins with a choice rather than a doctrine.\n\nThe Arabic indicates loving and preferring one thing over another. This is not a description of people who examined the evidence and concluded that no afterlife exists. It describes people who chose the immediate over the eventual.\n\nThat framing matters, because it makes the verse recognisable to anyone. Every person faces this preference constantly in small forms: the immediate pleasure against the long-term benefit, the easy gain against the honest one.\n\n\"And hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah\" is the second characteristic, and it moves from private preference to public harm. The Arabic indicates blocking and turning people away.\n\nIn Mecca this took concrete forms: intercepting travellers during the pilgrimage season to warn them against the Prophet, torturing slaves who accepted Islam, and eventually imposing a boycott.\n\n\"Striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked\" is the third, and it describes a subtler method. Rather than blocking the road, they misrepresent it. The Arabic suggests seeking to bend something straight, wanting it to appear distorted so that people avoid it by choice.\n\nBoth methods remain in use. Faith can be obstructed by force, and it can be obstructed by caricature, which is often more effective since it requires no violence and leaves the audience feeling they reached their own conclusion.\n\n\"It is they who have gone far astray.\" delivers the verdict. The Arabic indicates being lost at a great distance from the path.\n\nThe distance is worth noticing. Someone slightly off course can be redirected easily. These people are described as having gone far, which follows naturally from the three characteristics: preferring this world sets the direction, blocking others accelerates the departure, and distorting the path removes the possibility of finding the way back by accident.",
  "4": "\"We have not sent a messenger except in the language of his people to clarify ˹the message˺ for them.\" states a principle that governed every revelation in history, and its reasoning is entirely practical.\n\nA message must be understood to be a message at all. Sending a prophet who spoke an unfamiliar language would have created an obstacle rather than a mercy, and it would have given people a genuine excuse.\n\nThe stated purpose is clarification. The Arabic indicates making things clear, explaining until the meaning is plain. This is what a messenger does: he does not merely deliver words, he explains them, answers questions, and demonstrates the meaning in his conduct.\n\nThis principle has an implication for Muslims today that is worth drawing out. The Quran was revealed in Arabic because that was the language of its first audience. The purpose behind that choice, comprehension, still applies to everyone who reads it. Reciting without understanding fulfils part of the practice and misses what the language was chosen for.\n\n\"Then Allah leaves whoever He wills to stray and guides whoever He wills.\" addresses what happens after the message has been made clear.\n\nThis statement needs to be read alongside what the Quran says elsewhere, and the preceding chapters supplied it. Guidance is described as coming to whoever turns toward Allah, and straying is described as the settled consequence of persistent refusal. Allah's will governs both outcomes, and human choice is genuine and accountable.\n\nHolding both together is the position the Quran consistently maintains. It refuses to say that people are helpless, and it refuses to say that guidance is a human achievement.\n\n\"And He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" closes with two names that answer the question this raises. His might means the outcome is entirely in His hands, and His wisdom means it is never arbitrary. Whatever He decides is placed exactly where it belongs.",
  "5": "\"Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs, ˹ordering him,˺ \"Lead your people out of darkness and into light\"\" gives Moses the same mission described for the Prophet Muhammad at the opening of this chapter, in almost identical words.\n\nThe repetition is the point. Two messengers separated by centuries were sent with the same task, and the darkness they addressed was of the same kind: idol worship, injustice, and the confusion that follows when people lose sight of their Creator.\n\nFor the Children of Israel, the darkness was also literal oppression. They were enslaved, their sons were being killed, and their situation had no visible route out. The message brought to them was both spiritual and practical, since Moses came to lead them out of Egypt as well as out of disbelief.\n\n\"And remind them of Allah's days ˹of favour˺.\" adds a specific instruction, and the Arabic phrase is worth examining.\n\nThe expression indicates the days of Allah, and commentators have explained it as the notable days in which His power and favour were displayed: days of rescue, of blessing, and also of punishment for those who refused. For the Children of Israel these included the parting of the sea, the food provided in the wilderness, and water brought from rock.\n\nReminding people of such days is a form of teaching by memory rather than by argument. A community that remembers what it was rescued from tends to behave differently from one that has forgotten.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for whoever is steadfast, grateful.\" names the two qualities that allow a person to benefit from these reminders.\n\nThe pairing is precise and it covers both halves of life. Patience is what hardship requires; gratitude is what ease requires. Together they describe a believer who responds correctly whatever his circumstances, which is exactly what the Prophet described in the hadith recorded by Muslim about the affair of the believer being entirely good.",
  "6": "\"˹Consider˺ when Moses said to his people, \"Remember Allah's favour upon you when He rescued you from the people of Pharaoh\"\" shows Moses carrying out the instruction he had just been given. He was told to remind them of Allah's days, and this is him doing it.\n\nThe favour named is rescue, and it is placed first before any of the details. Whatever else happened, the central fact is that they were taken out of a situation they could not have escaped by themselves.\n\n\"Who afflicted you with dreadful torment—slaughtering your sons and keeping your women.\" describes what they were rescued from, and Moses does not soften it.\n\nThe killing of their sons was a policy of population control, described elsewhere in the Quran as Pharaoh dividing the people into factions and oppressing one group by killing their sons. The phrase about keeping the women indicates that the females were left alive, which in that context meant retained for servitude.\n\nThere is a reason Moses states this so plainly rather than moving quickly to gratitude. People forget what they have been delivered from with remarkable speed. A generation that grew up after the rescue would know the story without feeling it, and even those who lived through it settle into the new normal within a few years.\n\n\"That was a severe test from your Lord.\" names what the whole experience was.\n\nThe Arabic word for test can indicate a trial of hardship or a trial of favour, and commentators have applied it here both to the affliction and to the rescue. Both are tests. Suffering tests whether a person holds on; deliverance tests whether he remembers.\n\nThat double meaning fits the Children of Israel's subsequent history exactly. They passed through the sea and then asked for an idol, which the Quran records. The severe test did not end when the water closed behind them.",
  "7": "\"And ˹remember˺ when your Lord proclaimed, 'If you are grateful, I will certainly give you more.'\" contains one of the most quoted promises in the Quran, and the way it is introduced gives it weight.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates a formal announcement, a proclamation made publicly. This is not a passing remark but a declaration.\n\nThe promise itself is stated with emphasis in the original: gratitude will certainly be met with increase. What that increase consists of is deliberately left unspecified. Commentators have noted that this openness is part of the promise. It may come as more provision, more strength, more contentment, or more guidance, and often it comes in a form the person did not expect.\n\nThere is a practical dimension to this that anyone can observe. Gratitude changes what a person notices. Someone in the habit of recognising what he has been given tends to see more of it, while someone focused on what is missing sees more of that. The Quran is describing something real rather than merely encouraging good manners.\n\nGratitude in Islamic teaching is not only a feeling. Scholars describe it as involving the heart, which acknowledges the Giver, the tongue, which expresses it, and the limbs, which use the blessing in a way that pleases Him. A person who thanks Allah for his health and then uses that health in disobedience has completed only part of the act.\n\n\"But if you are ungrateful, surely My punishment is severe.\" gives the other side, and the phrasing is worth noticing.\n\nThe two halves are not symmetrical. Where gratitude is met with a promise of increase stated directly, ingratitude is answered with a warning about punishment rather than a threat of immediate loss.\n\nThe Arabic word for ingratitude here shares its root with disbelief, and the shared root is instructive. Both involve covering something over: one covers a gift, the other covers the Giver.",
  "8": "\"\"If you along with everyone on earth were to be ungrateful, then ˹know that˺ Allah is indeed Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\" completes Moses' address to his people, and it removes a misunderstanding that the previous verse might have left.\n\nSomeone hearing that gratitude brings increase and ingratitude brings punishment might imagine that Allah needs the gratitude, the way a person feels slighted when a favour goes unacknowledged.\n\nThe verse rules this out completely, and it does so by taking the situation to its extreme. Not one person's ingratitude, but the ingratitude of every human being on earth, all at once. The result would be nothing.\n\n\"Self-Sufficient\" translates the Arabic \"Ghani,\" which indicates being completely free of need, lacking nothing and depending on nothing. Worship adds nothing to Him and refusal takes nothing from Him.\n\n\"Praiseworthy\" translates \"Hamid,\" and its placement here is the more striking of the two. He remains praised and worthy of praise regardless of whether anyone praises Him. His worthiness is a fact about Him, not a status conferred by an audience.\n\nTogether these two names answer the question of who benefits from gratitude. The answer, consistent throughout the Quran, is the one who is grateful.\n\nA sacred hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim expresses this with unusual force, where Allah tells His servants that if the first and last of them, humans and jinn together, were as pious as the most pious heart among them, it would add nothing to His dominion, and if they were as wicked as the most wicked heart among them, it would diminish nothing from it.\n\nUnderstanding this changes the character of worship. A person who thinks he is doing Allah a service prays with a sense of credit accumulating. A person who understands this verse prays knowing that the entire arrangement is a favour extended to him.",
  "9": "\"Have you not ˹already˺ received the stories of those who were before you: the people of Noah, 'Âd, Thamûd, and those after them?\" appeals to history that the audience already knew.\n\nThese three communities appear repeatedly in the Quran, and their accounts were given at length in Surah Hud. The Arabs of Mecca knew the ruins of Ad and Thamud, since their trade routes passed nearby.\n\n\"Only Allah knows how many they were.\" acknowledges a limit to human knowledge in an honest way. Countless communities have risen and disappeared, most of them leaving no record at all. Archaeology recovers fragments; the full number is known only to Allah.\n\n\"Their messengers came to them with clear proofs\" states what each of them received. The Arabic indicates clear evidences that remove excuse.\n\n\"But they put their hands over their mouths\" describes their reaction with a gesture, and commentators have discussed what it means, offering several readings. Some understood it as placing their hands over the messengers' mouths to silence them. Others read it as biting their own hands in rage. Others explained it as a gesture of dismissal or mockery, or as covering their own mouths to indicate refusal to speak or listen.\n\nThe variety of explanations is worth acknowledging rather than pretending to certainty. What is clear from every reading is refusal expressed physically rather than through argument.\n\n\"And said, \"We totally reject what you have been sent with, and we are certainly in alarming doubt about what you are inviting us to.\"\" gives their words.\n\nThere is a contradiction inside their statement that repays attention. They announce total rejection and then describe themselves as being in unsettling doubt. A person genuinely uncertain does not begin by declaring complete refusal. Their doubt was disturbing enough to unsettle them, and their response was to shut the matter down rather than investigate it.",
  "10": "\"Their messengers asked ˹them˺, \"Is there any doubt about Allah, the Originator of the heavens and the earth?\"\" is the reply to a people who had just declared themselves in unsettling doubt.\n\nThe question is not dismissive. It points to the one thing that should be beyond dispute. Whatever uncertainties a person carries about details, the existence of the One who brought the heavens and earth into being from nothing is not among the difficult questions.\n\nThe Arabic word \"fatir\" indicates originating something, bringing it into existence without any prior model. It suggests creation from nothing rather than the shaping of existing material.\n\n\"He is inviting you in order to forgive your sins\" reframes the whole encounter. The messengers are not describing a summons to judgement. They are describing an invitation whose purpose is pardon.\n\nThat is a significant point about how the prophets presented their message. The call was not primarily a threat but an offer, and the offer was forgiveness for what had already been done.\n\n\"And delay your end until your appointed term.\" adds the worldly benefit. Responding brings respite, allowing a community to live out its natural span rather than being cut short. This matches what Hud told his people about rain and strength, and what Noah promised in similar terms.\n\n\"They argued, \"You are no more than humans like us!\"\" is the first objection, and it is the one every messenger faced.\n\n\"You ˹only˺ wish to turn us away from what our forefathers worshipped.\" is the second, and it names the real attachment. Not evidence, but inheritance.\n\n\"So bring us some compelling proof.\" is the third, demanding a decisive authority of their own choosing.\n\nThe three together form a complete refusal. Reject the messenger for being human, defend the religion for being old, and demand a miracle to settle a question that inheritance has already settled in their minds.",
  "11": "\"\"We are ˹indeed˺ only humans like you\"\" is the messengers' reply, and it begins by conceding the objection completely.\n\nTheir opponents had said that these were merely men, and the messengers agree. They do not claim to be angels, to possess hidden powers, or to be made of different material. Joseph's father and Noah before him had said the same, and the Prophet Muhammad was instructed to declare that he was only a man like them, receiving revelation.\n\nConceding the point removes the argument entirely. The Meccans and every earlier community had treated the messenger's humanity as a disqualification. The messengers treat it as simply true and irrelevant.\n\n\"But Allah favours whoever He chooses of His servants.\" explains what actually distinguishes them. Not a different nature, but a favour granted.\n\nThe Arabic indicates bestowing a gift, and it locates prophethood entirely in Allah's choosing rather than in the recipient's merit. This is why prophets in the Quran consistently describe their position as a mercy given to them, as Noah and Salih both did.\n\n\"It is not for us to bring you any proof without Allah's permission.\" answers the second demand, which was for a compelling sign. No messenger ever produced a miracle by his own decision. Moses did not choose when his staff would transform, and the Prophet Muhammad could not deliver the signs the Quraysh requested.\n\nThis is a genuine limit rather than an excuse, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated the same principle a few verses earlier.\n\n\"And in Allah let the believers put their trust.\" closes with an instruction that extends beyond the conversation.\n\nThe messengers had just admitted that they are ordinary men without independent power. That admission could sound like weakness, and the verse turns it into the opposite. Being unable to produce miracles on demand is not a problem for someone whose reliance is on the One who can.\n\nReliance is the natural conclusion of everything they have just said. If they are only men, and if all authority belongs to Allah, then trusting Him is not a pious sentiment but simple accuracy.",
  "12": "\"\"Why should we not put our trust in Allah, when He has truly guided us to ˹the very best of˺ ways?\" is a question rather than a statement, and the messengers use it to make reliance sound like the obvious position it is.\n\nThey are pointing to evidence from their own experience. Allah has already guided them, which is the greatest favour a person can receive. Someone who has been shown the road has grounds for trusting the One who showed it.\n\nThere is a practical logic here worth extracting. Trust in Allah is often treated as a leap taken in the dark. The messengers present it as a conclusion drawn from what has already happened. A person who looks back over his life and notices how he was led, protected, and provided for has a basis for confidence about what is ahead.\n\n\"Indeed, we will patiently endure whatever harm you may cause us.\" is the second half, and its placement is significant.\n\nImmediately after affirming their trust, they announce their expectation of being hurt. Reliance on Allah is not a claim that nothing bad will happen. The messengers expect harm and commit in advance to bearing it.\n\nThis corrects a serious misunderstanding about trust in Allah. It is not a strategy for avoiding hardship, and it does not promise protection from opposition. Every prophet named in these chapters suffered, and several were driven out or threatened with death.\n\nThe Arabic word for their endurance is \"sabr,\" and it means holding oneself firm rather than passively accepting. They are not surrendering to their opponents; they are committing to continue.\n\n\"And in Allah let the faithful put their trust.\" repeats the instruction from the previous verse, and the repetition gives it the weight of a refrain.\n\nTwo verses in a row end with the same call, addressed not to the opponents but to the believers. In the middle of a hostile exchange, the messengers keep turning to encourage the small group standing with them.",
  "13": "\"The disbelievers then threatened their messengers, \"We will certainly expel you from our land, unless you return to our faith.\"\" is where the argument ends and the coercion begins.\n\nThis is the standard progression in these accounts. Objections are raised, answered, and then abandoned in favour of force. Once a position cannot be defended, removing the person defending the other side becomes the remaining option.\n\nThe threat of expulsion was severe in that world. A man driven out of his community lost his protection, his livelihood, and often his life, since a person without a tribe could be attacked with no consequence.\n\nNotice the alternative offered: return to our faith. They describe conversion as a return, as though the messengers had wandered off from the correct position rather than been sent to correct it. The Quran records the Meccans using similar language.\n\nThere is also an assumption buried in the ultimatum that reveals how they thought about religion. Belief, in their view, was a matter of communal loyalty rather than truth. Change your position and you may stay; keep it and you leave. Nothing in the threat addresses whether the message was true.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad faced this exact pattern. He was eventually driven from Mecca, and Surah Muhammad notes how many towns were stronger than the one that expelled him.\n\n\"So their Lord revealed to them, \"We will surely destroy the wrongdoers\"\" is the response, and it comes as revelation to the messengers rather than as an argument to the opponents.\n\nThe timing matters. The threat is met with a private assurance to the ones being threatened, which is exactly what a person under pressure needs. The opponents hear nothing; the messengers are told how this ends.\n\nThe word for those to be destroyed is \"zalimin,\" the wrongdoers, which the Quran uses for those who put things where they do not belong.",
  "14": "\"And make you reside in the land after them.\" completes the promise begun in the previous verse, and it reverses the threat exactly.\n\nThey had said they would expel the messengers from the land. Allah's response is that the messengers will live in that land after those who threatened them are gone.\n\nThis is the pattern that played out repeatedly. Noah's people were removed and the believers inherited the earth. Pharaoh's forces drowned and the Children of Israel were settled. The Prophet Muhammad was driven from Mecca and returned to it eight years later as its people opened the gates.\n\n\"This is for whoever is in awe of standing before Me and fears My warning.\" states the condition attached to the promise, and it is worth examining because it is easy to misread.\n\nThe promise is not given to a particular ethnic group or to anyone who simply claims faith. It is attached to two qualities, both concerning a person's inner state.\n\nThe first is awe at the prospect of standing before Allah. The Arabic indicates fear mixed with reverence, and the specific image is of standing in His presence for judgement. A person who genuinely expects that meeting behaves differently from one who does not.\n\nThe second is fear of His warning. Taking the threat seriously rather than treating it as words.\n\nWhat makes this condition significant is that both qualities are internal and unverifiable by anyone else. Inheritance of the land is promised on the basis of what happens inside a person, not on the basis of numbers, weapons, or organisation.\n\nThere is a sobering implication for any community that considers itself entitled to Allah's support. The promise follows the qualities, not the label. A group that abandons awe of the reckoning while claiming the promise has kept the claim and lost the condition, and the Quran shows several communities in exactly that position.",
  "15": "\"And both sides called for judgment\" describes the point at which the dispute was submitted for a decision.\n\nThe Arabic indicates seeking a decisive verdict, asking for a matter to be opened and settled. Commentators have understood this in two ways that both fit. The messengers asked Allah to judge between them and their people, which prophets in the Quran do at the end of long resistance. And the opponents themselves demanded the outcome, mockingly challenging their messengers to bring on what they had threatened, exactly as Noah's people and Ad had done.\n\nBoth sides wanted the matter resolved, and each was confident about the result. Only one could be right, which is precisely what makes such moments so decisive.\n\nThere is something worth noticing in the fact that the opponents participated in this. They were not judged after asking for mercy; they were judged after demanding judgement. Surah Yunus put the question directly to people in that position, asking whether the wicked realise what they are asking to be hastened.\n\n\"So every stubborn tyrant was doomed.\" gives the outcome in a single phrase, and the description of those destroyed is precise.\n\nTwo words are used in the Arabic. The first indicates a person who is obstinate, hostile to truth, refusing to yield even when the matter is clear. The second indicates one who is overbearing and tyrannical, exalting himself over others.\n\nThe pairing describes a recognisable type. Stubbornness alone produces a difficult person; combined with power over others it produces the figures that fill these accounts: Pharaoh, the chiefs of Ad, the elite of Midian.\n\nIt is also worth noticing what the verse does not say. It does not say that everyone who disagreed was destroyed. The description is specific, and it identifies those who both refused the truth and used their position to impose that refusal on others.",
  "16": "\"Awaiting them is Hell\" states the destination of those just described, and the Arabic construction indicates something lying ahead, waiting to be reached, the way a traveller has a destination in front of him.\n\nThat image is used deliberately in the Quran. Life is presented as a journey, and the question is never whether a person arrives somewhere but where.\n\n\"And they will be left to drink oozing pus\" describes what is provided there, and the Quran's directness here is uncomfortable by design.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a foul liquid, and commentators explained it as the discharge that comes from the bodies of the punished. Some early scholars described it as what flows from wounds and rotting flesh.\n\nThere is a reason for such an unpleasant detail, and it is not to shock for its own sake. Thirst is the most basic and urgent human need, and every description of Paradise in the Quran includes water, rivers, and pure drink. Placing the opposite here creates a direct contrast: those who receive rivers and those who receive this.\n\nThere is also a connection worth drawing to what such people pursued in life. Many of them spent their years chasing satisfaction of appetite, refusing every restraint on what they consumed and desired. What is described here is appetite that can never be satisfied by what is available.\n\nThe word for their drinking indicates that they will be made to drink it, which removes any suggestion of choice.\n\nSome readers find passages of this kind difficult, and the difficulty is worth addressing honestly rather than avoided. The Quran describes both outcomes in vivid terms because it is trying to produce a response while a response is still possible. A warning delivered in comfortable, abstract language is not a warning at all, and the same chapter that contains this verse also contains the promise of forgiveness extended to anyone who turns.",
  "17": "\"Which they will sip with difficulty, and can hardly swallow.\" continues the description from the previous verse, and it slows the scene down to a single repeated action.\n\nThe Arabic indicates taking it in small mouthfuls, forcing it down bit by bit. This is not a punishment delivered all at once but one that is experienced in slow, deliberate stages.\n\nThere is something particularly grim in the detail of near-swallowing. The drink does not go down. It is taken, resisted, and taken again, so that the act never completes and never ends.\n\n\"Death will overwhelm them from every side, yet they will not ˹be able to˺ die.\" describes the condition that surrounds this.\n\nThe Arabic indicates death coming at them from every direction, closing in from all sides. Everything about their state is what would normally kill a person, and death does not arrive.\n\nThis is one of the hardest images in the Quran, and its logic is worth understanding. Death, in ordinary experience, is the limit of suffering. Whatever a person endures, there is a point beyond which endurance ends. Removing that limit is what makes this punishment different in kind rather than merely in degree.\n\nThe Quran mentions elsewhere that those in the Fire will call out asking for their Lord to finish them, and will be told that they will remain. Surah Ta-Ha describes a person who neither dies there nor lives.\n\n\"Awaiting them still is harsher torment.\" closes the verse by indicating that what has been described is not the whole of it. Something more severe lies beyond.\n\nThe wording uses the same construction as the opening of the previous verse, describing what lies ahead of a traveller. Even in the middle of this description, there is a further destination.\n\nFor anyone weighing the choices this chapter sets out, the contrast could not be sharper: rivers and peace on one side, and this on the other.",
  "18": "\"The parable of the deeds of those who disbelieve in their Lord is that of ashes fiercely blown away by wind on a stormy day.\" offers an image that answers a question many people ask.\n\nWhat happens to the good deeds of someone who rejected Allah? A person may have been generous, honest in business, kind to his family, and helpful to strangers. The verse addresses exactly this.\n\nThe comparison is precise, and every element in it carries meaning. Ashes are what remains after something has burned, and they have the shape of what they came from while having none of its substance. A pile of ash looks solid until it is touched.\n\nThe wind is described as fierce, and the day as stormy, which removes any possibility of the ashes settling somewhere. Nothing is left to gather.\n\n\"They will gain nothing from what they have earned.\" states the outcome plainly. The Arabic indicates having no power over any of it, no ability to hold or benefit from what was accumulated.\n\nThis needs careful handling, because it raises a fair question about justice. Islamic teaching offers some clarity here. Good done by a disbeliever is not without any effect at all, and the Prophet mentioned in a hadith recorded by Muslim that Allah does not wrong a believer regarding a good deed, and that a disbeliever is given provision in this world for the good he does. What such deeds cannot do is produce reward in the Hereafter, because the foundation that gives deeds their eternal value is absent.\n\nThe image explains why. Ashes are real, and they are the residue of something that has already been consumed. Deeds performed without any relationship to Allah are similar: real actions, with their worldly effects, and nothing that survives.\n\n\"That is ˹truly˺ the farthest one can stray.\" names the tragedy: a lifetime of effort producing nothing at the point it was most needed.",
  "19": "\"Have you not seen that Allah created the heavens and the earth for a reason?\" asks a question about purpose, and the Arabic phrase indicates creation with truth, meaning for a genuine end rather than as a game or an accident.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's foundational claims, and everything else rests on it. If the universe exists without purpose, then morality is preference, justice is a human invention, and the accounting described in the previous verses would make no sense.\n\nThe Quran presses this argument in several places, asking whether people thought they were created without purpose and would not be returned, and stating that the heavens and earth and what is between them were not created in play.\n\nThe question format is an invitation to look. The order visible in creation, which the preceding chapters described at length, does not suggest something thrown together.\n\n\"If He wills, He can eliminate you and produce a new creation.\" draws the consequence, and it is a sobering one.\n\nHuman beings tend to assume their own necessity. A person imagines that the world requires him, that his community is permanent, that humanity as it currently exists is the point of everything. The verse removes that assumption entirely.\n\nThe One who created purposefully can replace what fails to serve the purpose. This is not a threat aimed only at individuals; the wording addresses whole populations.\n\nThe preceding chapters demonstrated this repeatedly. Communities that seemed permanent were removed, and others took their place. Hud told his people plainly that his Lord would replace them with others and they were not harming Him in the least.\n\nThere is a discipline in absorbing this. It removes the sense of entitlement that grows in prosperous communities, and it also removes despair, since a world with purpose is a world where nothing is wasted and nothing is arbitrary.",
  "20": "\"And that is not difficult for Allah ˹at all˺.\" is one of the shortest verses in this chapter, and it closes the thought begun in the verse before it.\n\nReplacing an entire creation with a new one sounds like an enormous undertaking, and that impression is exactly what this verse addresses. The Arabic indicates that such a thing is not mighty or burdensome for Allah.\n\nThe word choice matters. Something described as difficult implies effort, resistance, and the possibility of failure. None of these apply. What is impossible for us and trivial for Him are not different points on the same scale, since the scale itself is a human one.\n\nThe Quran states this in various ways throughout. Creation and resurrection are described as being no more than a single word, and Surah Yasin explains that His command, when He intends a thing, is only to say to it be, and it is.\n\nThere is a practical dimension to this brief verse that is easy to overlook. People instinctively rank their problems by size, and they pray accordingly. A small request feels reasonable; a large one feels like an imposition. This verse dissolves that ranking.\n\nIf replacing the whole of creation is not difficult, then nothing a person could ask for falls into a category of being too much. The Prophet encouraged this attitude in supplication, teaching that a person should ask with determination rather than saying that Allah may grant it if He wishes, since nothing can compel Him and nothing is too great for Him, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nPlaced here, immediately after a warning about replacement, the verse serves as a reminder to the people being warned. They were confident in their position because they could not imagine it changing. The verse tells them that changing it requires nothing at all.",
  "21": "\"They will all appear before Allah\" moves the scene to the Day of Judgement, and the Arabic indicates emerging into full view, coming out into the open.\n\nEverything that was hidden is now exposed. The private conversations, the real motives, and the relationships built on convenience are all visible.\n\n\"And the lowly ˹followers˺ will appeal to the arrogant ˹leaders˺\" describes the first exchange, and it is a conversation between people who spent their lives on the same side.\n\nThe Arabic distinguishes the weak from those who considered themselves great. This is the division that ran through every community in these chapters: the chiefs who opposed the messengers and the crowds who followed them.\n\n\"\"We were your ˹dedicated˺ followers, so will you ˹then˺ protect us from Allah's torment in any way?\"\" is the followers' appeal, and it rests on a claim of loyalty. We did what you said. Do something for us now.\n\nThe request is pitiful because it repeats the mistake that brought them there. Even at this point they are looking to the same people, asking human authority to shield them from divine judgement.\n\n\"They will reply, \"Had Allah guided us, we would have guided you.\"\" is the leaders' answer, and it is an excuse rather than an apology. They present themselves as equally lost, which sidesteps the fact that they actively turned others away.\n\n\"˹Now˺ it is all the same for us whether we suffer patiently or impatiently, there is no escape for us.\" completes their reply and abandons the followers entirely.\n\nThe observation about patience is worth noticing. In this life, patience under hardship produces reward and eases the burden. There, it changes nothing, because patience has value only where it is a choice made in hope.\n\nThe whole exchange demolishes the excuse of following. Everyone in that conversation had ears, and each will answer for what he chose to follow.",
  "22": "\"And Satan will say ˹to his followers˺ after the judgment has been passed\" introduces the second speech of that Day, and it is the most devastating of them all.\n\nHaving heard their leaders disown them, the followers now hear from the one who guided the whole enterprise.\n\n\"\"Indeed, Allah has made you a true promise. I too made you a promise, but I failed you.\"\" is his opening, and it is a confession. Everything Allah warned about turned out to be accurate. Everything Satan offered turned out to be empty.\n\n\"I did not have any authority over you.\" is the crucial admission, and it removes the excuse every one of them was preparing.\n\nThe Arabic indicates having no power, no compelling authority, no ability to force. He could not make anyone sin. Surah al-Hijr records him admitting that he had no power over Allah's sincere servants.\n\n\"I only called you, and you responded to me.\" describes what actually happened. An invitation was issued and accepted. The whole of his work consisted of suggestion, and the decision belonged to the one who acted on it.\n\nThis is one of the most important corrections in the Quran regarding responsibility. People routinely describe themselves as having been made to do something by temptation. On that Day, the tempter himself testifies that he only made the offer.\n\n\"So do not blame me; blame yourselves.\" is his verdict on them, and it is delivered by the last being any of them would want to hear it from.\n\n\"I cannot save you, nor can you save me.\" states the situation plainly, and it ends any imagined partnership.\n\n\"Indeed, I denounce your previous association of me with Allah ˹in loyalty˺.\" is his final disowning. He rejects the obedience they gave him.\n\n\"Surely the wrongdoers will suffer a painful punishment.\" closes his speech, and even Satan ends by stating the truth.",
  "23": "\"Those who believe and do good will be admitted into Gardens, under which rivers flow\" turns from the scenes of judgement to the other outcome, and the shift in tone is immediate.\n\nAfter the pus, the failed appeals, and Satan's speech, this verse arrives like an opening window.\n\nThe two conditions are the Quran's standard pairing: belief and righteous action. Neither functions without the other, and they appear together throughout the Book.\n\nThe description of flowing rivers is one the Quran uses constantly, and its force for the original audience was considerable. In a peninsula where water determined survival, permanent rivers were the clearest possible image of abundance without anxiety.\n\n\"To stay there forever\" removes the flaw that runs through every good thing in this life. Everything here ends, and knowing that shadows even great happiness.\n\n\"By the Will of their Lord\" is a phrase worth pausing on. Entry into Paradise is attributed to Allah's permission rather than presented as a payment earned. This matches what the Prophet said in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, that nobody's deeds will admit him to Paradise, including his own, unless Allah covers him with mercy. Deeds are the condition; mercy is the cause.\n\n\"Where they will be greeted with \"Peace!\"\" ends the verse with a single word, and it is the word Muslims exchange daily.\n\nThe Arabic \"salam\" indicates safety, security, and freedom from all harm. For people who have just passed through the reckoning, this is precisely the news that matters. Whatever they feared is finished.\n\nThere is something deliberate about ending this verse with a greeting rather than a description of pleasures. The Quran mentions the gardens, the rivers, and the permanence, and then closes with a word spoken between people.\n\nThe same greeting appeared in Surah ar-Ra'd, where the angels enter from every gate saying peace be upon them for their perseverance.",
  "24": "\"Do you not see how Allah compares a good word to a good tree?\" introduces one of the most beautiful comparisons in the Quran, and the question invites the listener to look at something familiar.\n\nThe Arabic phrase for a good word, \"kalimah tayyibah,\" has been explained by commentators in several complementary ways. Many identified it specifically with the declaration of faith, the statement that there is no god but Allah. Others took it more broadly to include every good and true statement: sincere belief, honest speech, kind words, and remembrance of Allah.\n\nBoth readings work, and the second grows naturally out of the first, since all good speech traces back to that foundation.\n\n\"Its root is firm\" describes the first quality, and it names the part nobody sees.\n\nA tree's stability comes from below ground. The visible trunk and branches depend entirely on a root system that no observer notices. Faith works the same way: what holds a person steady in a crisis is something built quietly over years, invisible to everyone around him.\n\n\"And its branches reach the sky\" describes the second quality, and it points upward.\n\nA deep root allows height. The two are connected in an actual tree, since a shallow-rooted plant cannot support a large canopy without falling. Applied to a person, inner conviction produces outward effect: words, actions, and influence that reach far beyond the individual.\n\nThe comparison also answers something about how faith operates in a life. It is not a decoration attached to a person but an organism with a structure, drawing from below and producing above.\n\nThere is a further detail worth noticing. The Prophet asked his companions about a tree resembling a Muslim, and Ibn Umar thought of the date palm but stayed silent out of shyness, as recorded in al-Bukhari and Muslim. The date palm gives constantly, uses every part of itself, and stands in conditions that kill other plants.",
  "25": "\"˹Always˺ yielding its fruit in every season by the Will of its Lord.\" completes the description of the good tree, and it adds the quality that makes the comparison practical.\n\nMost trees produce in a season and then stop. This one gives continuously, and the Arabic indicates that it does so at all times, by the permission of its Lord.\n\nApplied to the good word, this describes something recognisable. A person whose faith is genuine produces benefit constantly rather than in bursts. There is no season in which he is unavailable, no period during which nothing comes from him. Prayer, honesty, patience, kindness, and useful work continue through good years and difficult ones.\n\nThere is also a dimension that reaches beyond a person's lifetime. The Prophet said, in a hadith recorded by Muslim, that when a person dies his deeds end except for three: ongoing charity, knowledge that benefits others, and a righteous child who prays for him. A good word planted in someone's heart can keep producing fruit for generations after the one who spoke it is gone.\n\nThe phrase \"by the Will of its Lord\" prevents a misreading. The tree does not produce by its own power. Fruit comes when Allah permits it, which keeps the credit where it belongs and also explains why sincere effort sometimes seems to produce nothing visible.\n\n\"This is how Allah sets forth parables for the people, so perhaps they will be mindful.\" states why such comparisons are given.\n\nThe Arabic word for these parables indicates similitudes, examples drawn from what people already know in order to explain what they do not.\n\nThis is a teaching method the Quran uses constantly, and it reflects something about how understanding works. Abstract truths slide off the mind; images stay. A person who has seen a tree with deep roots and reaching branches has been given a picture he can carry, and it will explain faith to him more effectively than a definition.",
  "26": "\"And the parable of an evil word is that of an evil tree, uprooted from the earth, having no stability.\" completes the comparison by describing the opposite, and it is deliberately brief.\n\nThe good tree received two verses; this one receives a single line. That asymmetry is part of the point. There is less to say about something with no depth.\n\nThe Arabic phrase for the evil word covers disbelief, false claims about Allah, lies, slander, and speech that spreads harm. Just as the good word was identified by many commentators with the declaration of faith, this one is identified primarily with its rejection.\n\nThe tree it resembles is described as uprooted, torn out of the ground. Commentators have discussed whether it was pulled up after growing or never had roots at all, and both pictures serve the comparison. Some described a plant like the colocynth, which spreads on the surface of the desert without any depth beneath it.\n\n\"Having no stability\" states the consequence. Nothing holds it.\n\nThe contrast with the previous verses is exact at every point. The good tree has firm roots; this one has none. The good tree reaches upward; this one lies on the surface. The good tree produces continuously; this one produces nothing worth mentioning.\n\nThere is an observation here that anyone can verify. Falsehood is often loud and briefly impressive, and it collapses as soon as real pressure arrives. Surah ar-Ra'd made the same point with a different image, comparing falsehood to the foam that rides on a flood and vanishes while the water soaks into the ground.\n\nThe practical warning concerns where a person invests. Everyone is planting something with their words, their habits, and their commitments. The question the comparison poses is whether any of it has roots, and the honest test is what remains when circumstances become difficult.",
  "27": "\"Allah makes the believers steadfast with the firm Word ˹of faith˺ in this worldly life and the Hereafter.\" states the outcome of everything the tree comparison has described.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making something firm, fixing it in place so that it does not shift. The believers are not described as making themselves steadfast; Allah does it, and He does it by means of the firm word.\n\nThat word is the same good word from the earlier verses, the declaration of faith and everything that grows from it. What holds a person steady in a crisis is the root system built over years of holding to it.\n\n\"In this worldly life and the Hereafter\" identifies two moments where such firmness is needed, and the Sunnah connects the second directly to a specific scene.\n\nThe Prophet explained this verse as referring to the questioning in the grave, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim. When a person is questioned about his Lord, his religion, and his prophet, the believer answers with certainty, and this is described as Allah making him firm with the firm word.\n\nThe steadfastness in this life is equally real. It appears when someone loses a child and does not lose his faith, when a person under pressure to compromise refuses, and when a believer keeps praying through a long period in which nothing seems to be answered. Jacob and Joseph both illustrated this at length in the chapter that came a little earlier.\n\n\"And Allah leaves the wrongdoers to stray.\" states the other outcome, using the term for those who put things where they do not belong.\n\n\"For Allah does what He wills.\" closes the verse with a statement that prevents any objection.\n\nThere is no court above His decision and no standard external to Him by which it could be measured. This is the same principle that closed the discussion of the Fire in Surah Hud, where the chapter ended by affirming that the Lord does what He intends.",
  "28": "\"Have you not seen those ˹disbelievers˺ who meet Allah's favours with ingratitude\" turns from the general comparison of trees to a specific group, and the wording invites the listener to look at people he can identify.\n\nThe Arabic phrase is striking: they exchanged the favour of Allah for ingratitude, or for disbelief, since the word carries both meanings. Something given was traded for its opposite.\n\nCommentators have discussed who is meant. Many identified them with the leaders of Quraysh, who had received enormous favours: the sanctuary at Mecca, the security of a city where others were raided, the prosperity of the trade caravans, and finally a messenger sent from among themselves. Every one of these is mentioned elsewhere in the Quran as a favour to them.\n\nThe greatest of these gifts was the Prophet himself, and their response to it was rejection. Surah Ali Imran describes the sending of a messenger from among the believers as a favour bestowed, and Quraysh had received it directly.\n\n\"And lead their own people to their doom?\" is the second half, and it adds the harm they caused others.\n\nThe Arabic indicates causing them to descend into the abode of ruin. This is the language of leadership, and it repeats the image used of Pharaoh in Surah Hud, who goes before his people on the Day of Judgement and leads them into the Fire.\n\nThose they led were their own people, which makes it worse. These were relatives, dependants, and fellow tribesmen who trusted them. The Meccan leaders who campaigned against the message took a great many people with them, some of whom died at Badr still following.\n\nThere is a warning in this for anyone with influence over others, and the scale of the influence does not change the principle. Followers are not an achievement; they are people who will arrive wherever they are taken.",
  "29": "\"In Hell they will burn.\" states the destination of the leaders and those they led, and it is stated without elaboration.\n\nThe brevity is deliberate. This chapter has already described the drink of pus, the near-swallowing, and the death that surrounds without arriving. Nothing more needs to be added here, and the short sentence lands harder for its plainness.\n\nThe Arabic indicates entering and being scorched by it, experiencing its heat directly rather than merely being placed nearby.\n\n\"What an evil place for settlement.\" completes the verse with a word that carries bitter irony.\n\nThe Arabic term indicates a place where one settles and remains, a residence. The same root produces words for stability and permanence. This is the language a person uses about a home he has finally established after years of travelling.\n\nApplying it here is deliberate. The leaders described in the previous verse had spent their lives building a position: wealth, standing, influence over their people, a city they controlled. Everything they constructed was aimed at a secure place to settle.\n\nThey arrive at one, and this is it.\n\nThere is a pattern in these chapters of describing punishment with the vocabulary of arrival and reward. Pharaoh leads his people to a watering place that turns out to be fire. The curse that follows the wrongdoers is called a gift given to them. Here the destination is called a settlement.\n\nThe effect of this language is to make the reader feel the reversal rather than simply be told about it. What people work toward and what they reach are not always the same thing, and the words used for the journey do not change at the end.\n\nFor those who followed such leaders, the verse offers no separate category. They arrive at the same place, which is what the exchange between the weak and the arrogant a few verses earlier already established.",
  "30": "\"They set up equals to Allah to mislead ˹others˺ from His Way.\" names what these leaders actually did, and the Arabic word for what they established is worth examining.\n\nThe term indicates rivals, peers, things treated as equivalent to something else. They did not usually claim that their idols created the heavens; the Meccans acknowledged Allah as creator. What they did was treat other things as deserving what belongs only to Him: worship, ultimate trust, and the authority to make things lawful or forbidden.\n\nThat is what setting up an equal means in practice, and understanding it this way makes the warning relevant far beyond carved stones. Anything given the place that belongs to Allah becomes a rival in exactly this sense.\n\nThe stated purpose is misleading others. This is the third time in a few verses that the harm done to other people has been emphasised, and the pattern is deliberate. These men are not condemned merely for their own beliefs but for what they did with their influence.\n\n\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Enjoy yourselves!\"\" is the instruction given in response, and it is one of the sharpest expressions in the Quran.\n\nIt is not permission. It is a statement made with heavy irony, telling them to carry on with what they are doing, since the outcome is already known. The Arabic imperative here functions as a warning dressed as an invitation.\n\nThere is a particular force in this form of address. Argument has been offered repeatedly throughout the chapter, and refused. What remains is to let them proceed, which is the same conclusion reached in Surah Hud when the Prophet was told to say that they should persist in their way while the believers persisted in theirs.\n\n\"Surely your destination is the Fire.\" completes the sentence and removes the ambiguity entirely.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the place one comes back to, the final return. Whatever the enjoyment consists of, it has a fixed endpoint, and the verse names it without softening.",
  "31": "\"Tell My believing servants to establish prayer and donate from what We have provided for them\" turns from warning the deniers to instructing the believers, and the shift in address is warm.\n\nThey are called My believing servants, which places them in a direct relationship with Allah, in contrast to the leaders described in the preceding verses who set up rivals to Him.\n\nTwo practices are named, and they are the two the Quran pairs most often. Prayer is the connection upward; giving is the connection outward. A religion consisting only of the first becomes private piety with no social effect, and one consisting only of the second becomes charity with no anchor.\n\nThe verb for prayer indicates establishing it, keeping it upright and regular, rather than merely performing it occasionally.\n\nThe phrase \"from what We have provided for them\" appeared in similar form in Surah ar-Ra'd, and it carries the same reminder. What is given away was never independently owned.\n\n\"Openly and secretly\" includes both forms of giving deliberately. Secret charity protects the recipient's dignity and guards the giver's sincerity. Open giving encourages others and establishes the practice in a community. A person with judgement knows which situation calls for which, and the Quran does not rank them absolutely.\n\n\"Before the arrival of a Day in which there will be no ransom or friendly connections.\" explains the urgency, and it names the two things people normally rely on when they are in trouble.\n\nThe first is payment. Money resolves an extraordinary range of human problems, and the verse states that this one will not be among them. Surah Yunus made the same point about a wrongdoer who would ransom himself with everything on earth.\n\nThe second is connections. Knowing the right person opens doors everywhere, and in a tribal society this was the primary form of security.\n\nBoth are removed, and what remains is what a person sent ahead: the prayers established and the wealth given.",
  "32": "\"It is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth and sends down rain from the sky, causing fruits to grow as a provision for you.\" begins a list of favours, and it follows directly from the instruction to give. Recognising where provision comes from makes parting with some of it far easier.\n\nThe sequence described is worth noticing. Rain falls, fruit grows, and people eat. Three stages, and human beings control none of them. A farmer can plant and tend, and he cannot make a single drop fall.\n\n\"He has subjected the ships for your service, sailing through the sea by His command\" moves to something people build themselves, and this is where the verse becomes more interesting.\n\nA ship is a human construction. Wood is cut, shaped, and assembled by craftsmen. Yet the verse attributes the sailing to Allah's command, and the reason becomes clear on reflection. The ship floats because water behaves in a particular way. It moves because wind blows or currents run. It arrives because the sea permits it that day.\n\nEvery one of those conditions was arranged, and none was designed by the shipbuilder. Human effort works only inside a system it did not create, and the Arabic word for subjecting indicates something made available and set at one's disposal.\n\nFor the Meccans this had immediate relevance. Their prosperity depended on trade, including sea routes, and anyone who has sailed knows how completely conditions govern the outcome.\n\n\"And has subjected the rivers for you.\" adds the inland equivalent. Rivers carry water to places rain does not reach, allow irrigation, and support settlement and transport.\n\nThe whole verse traces a chain from the sky to the table and from the sea to the market, and at every point it names who arranged it. The point is not that people do nothing, but that everything they do rests on an arrangement they inherited without asking.",
  "33": "\"He has ˹also˺ subjected for you the sun and the moon, both constantly orbiting\" continues the list of favours, and it moves from the sea to the sky.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that both are continuously moving in their courses, persistent and unceasing. This constancy is what makes them useful. A sun that rose irregularly would be a spectacle rather than a foundation for life.\n\nThe sun provides the energy on which every living thing depends. Plants grow by it, water is lifted by it, and the temperature that allows human life is maintained by it. The moon governs tides and provided the calendar by which the Arabs, like most ancient peoples, measured months.\n\nThe word for subjecting is worth understanding correctly. It does not mean human beings command these bodies. It means they have been made to serve human needs, placed in an arrangement that benefits people who had no part in setting it up.\n\nThis distinction matters, because ancient peoples frequently worshipped the sun and moon. The Quran repeatedly redirects that impulse, and Surah Fussilat instructs people not to prostrate to the sun or the moon but to the One who created them.\n\n\"And has subjected the day and night for you.\" completes the pair.\n\nThe alternation of light and darkness structures human life completely. Work becomes possible in one and rest in the other, and the body's internal rhythms are tuned to the cycle. Surah Yunus described the night as a time to rest in and the day as bright, and Surah an-Naba calls sleep a means of rest and the day a means of livelihood.\n\nWhat ties this verse to the instruction about charity a few verses earlier is the sheer scale of what has been provided without being asked for. A person weighing whether to give away part of his wealth is being reminded that the sun rises for him daily and he was never billed for it.",
  "34": "\"And He has granted you all that you asked Him for.\" states something so large that it requires careful reading.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that He gave you from everything you asked Him for, and commentators have explained it in two complementary ways. He has given from everything a person requested, and He has given what people need whether or not they thought to ask.\n\nThe second reading is the more striking. Human beings receive air, functioning organs, sunlight, and the arrangement of the seasons without ever having requested any of it. Most of what sustains a life was provided before the person was capable of forming a request.\n\nThis should not be misread as a promise that every specific prayer is granted in the form it was made. The Quran and the Sunnah are clear that answers come in different ways, and the Prophet described supplication being answered immediately, stored for the Hereafter, or used to avert a harm, in a hadith recorded by Ahmad.\n\n\"If you tried to count Allah's blessings, you would never be able to number them.\" is the statement that follows, and it is worth testing rather than merely accepting.\n\nTry to count the blessings involved in a single breath: the air, the lungs, the muscles, the oxygen exchange, the heart circulating it, and the nervous system running the whole process without supervision. One breath cannot be fully counted, and a person takes thousands each day.\n\n\"Indeed humankind is truly unfair, ˹totally˺ ungrateful.\" delivers the assessment, and both words are intense forms in the Arabic.\n\nThe charge of being unfair indicates putting things where they do not belong: receiving from One and thanking another, or receiving constantly and acknowledging nothing.\n\nThe charge of ingratitude uses a word built on the root meaning to cover over. A person surrounded by uncountable gifts manages to cover them all, and complains about what is missing.",
  "35": "\"˹Remember˺ when Abraham prayed, \"My Lord! Make this city ˹of Mecca˺ secure\"\" begins a long supplication that occupies the next several verses, and it comes from the man who built the sanctuary the Meccans were so proud of.\n\nPlacing this here is deliberate. The chapter has been addressing people who received extraordinary favours and met them with ingratitude, and one of the greatest of those favours was the security of their city. The Quran now shows them where that security came from: a prayer made by their ancestor.\n\nThe request for safety was not abstract. Mecca sat in a barren valley on trade routes crossed by raiding tribes, and it became a sanctuary where fighting was prohibited and travellers were unmolested. Surah al-Qasas records the Meccans themselves acknowledging that they had been given a safe sanctuary while people around them were being snatched away.\n\n\"And keep me and my children away from the worship of idols.\" is the second request, and it is the more remarkable of the two.\n\nAbraham is the man who broke the idols of his people, argued with his father about them, and was thrown into a fire for refusing them. If anyone on earth was secure from idol worship, it was him.\n\nHe prayed for protection from it anyway.\n\nThis tells us something important about how the prophets understood the human heart. Past resistance guarantees nothing about the future, and confidence in one's own steadfastness is itself a danger. Joseph said something similar when he admitted he might yield to temptation without Allah's help.\n\nThere is a report from Ibrahim al-Taymi, preserved in the commentary literature, asking who can feel safe from trial after Abraham prayed this prayer.\n\nThe inclusion of his children extends the concern beyond himself. A parent who takes faith seriously worries about what happens after he is gone, and this is exactly the anxiety the prayer addresses.",
  "36": "\"\"My Lord! They have caused many people to go astray.\" explains why Abraham asked for protection from idols in the previous verse.\n\nHe attributes the leading astray to the idols themselves, which is a way of speaking about what they caused rather than what they did. Objects of stone cannot act, and the Quran makes that point repeatedly. What they can do is become the occasion for whole nations wandering off, generation after generation.\n\nAbraham had seen this in his own family and city. His father made and sold such images, and the argument between them is recorded in several chapters.\n\n\"So whoever follows me is with me\" is the first half of his response, and it is a simple statement of belonging.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that such a person is of him, part of him. This is a definition of kinship that has nothing to do with blood, and the Quran develops it elsewhere by stating that the people most deserving of Abraham are those who follow him, along with this Prophet and those who believe.\n\nThat principle mattered enormously in Mecca, where the Quraysh claimed Abraham as their ancestor while filling his sanctuary with the very things he prayed to be kept away from. Descent from him gave them nothing.\n\n\"And whoever disobeys me—then surely You are ˹still˺ All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" is where the prayer becomes extraordinary.\n\nThe expected ending would be a request for such people to be punished, or at least a disowning of them. Abraham does not finish the sentence that way. He leaves them with Allah's forgiveness and mercy.\n\nThis reflects the character the Quran attributes to him elsewhere, describing him as forbearing, tender-hearted, and constantly turning to his Lord, in the passage where he pleaded for the people of Lot.\n\nThere is something instructive in the restraint. A man with every reason to condemn those who reject the truth ends his sentence by naming Allah's mercy instead.",
  "37": "\"\"Our Lord! I have settled some of my offspring in a barren valley, near Your Sacred House\"\" describes an act that must have been among the hardest of Abraham's life.\n\nHe had brought Hagar and the infant Ishmael to an empty valley with no water, no crops, and no inhabitants, and left them there on Allah's instruction. The account is preserved in a long hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, which describes Hagar asking whether Allah had commanded this, and, on hearing that He had, saying that He would not abandon them.\n\nThe Arabic phrase for the valley indicates one without cultivation, containing no plant life whatsoever. Anyone who has seen the terrain around Mecca understands how literal this description is.\n\n\"So that they may establish prayer.\" gives the reason for the whole arrangement, and it is worth pausing on.\n\nThe purpose was not trade, safety, or comfort. A family was placed in one of the least habitable places on earth so that prayer would be established there. Everything Mecca later became grew out of that single intention.\n\n\"So make the hearts of ˹believing˺ people incline towards them\" is the request for community. The Arabic indicates hearts yearning toward them, drawn in their direction.\n\nThe prayer was answered with remarkable precision. Millions travel to that valley every year, and the movement has continued for fourteen centuries. People arrive from every continent at considerable expense, drawn by exactly the inclination Abraham described.\n\n\"And provide them with fruits\" is the request for sustenance in a place that grows nothing.\n\nThis too was answered, and in a manner that fits the geography. Mecca produces almost no food, and it has been supplied for centuries by trade, by pilgrims, and now by imports from everywhere. A barren valley became one of the best-provisioned cities in the region without ever growing a crop.\n\n\"So perhaps they will be thankful.\" names the purpose of the provision.",
  "38": "\"\"Our Lord! You certainly know what we conceal and what we reveal.\"\" is a pause in the middle of Abraham's supplication, and it changes the character of everything around it.\n\nHe has just made a series of requests: security for the city, protection from idols, hearts inclining toward his family, and provision in a barren place. Now he acknowledges that the One being asked already knows the whole situation.\n\nThe Arabic covers both what is hidden and what is shown, which leaves nothing outside. This includes the anxieties he did not put into words, the fears about his family that a father carries silently, and the intentions behind each request.\n\nThere is something worth learning from this about how supplication works. A person praying to Allah is not informing Him of anything. The Prophet's own teaching reflects this, and the Quran describes Allah as nearer to a person than his jugular vein.\n\nIf Allah already knows, why ask at all? The answer given consistently in Islamic teaching is that asking is itself the point. Supplication is an act of worship, an admission of need, and a turning toward the One who provides. It changes the one who prays rather than informing the One who is asked.\n\nAbraham's acknowledgement also carries a note of trust. He has stated his requests, and he is effectively saying that whatever he has failed to express is known anyway.\n\n\"Nothing on earth or in heaven is hidden from Allah.\" extends the statement from his own case to everything that exists.\n\nThis is the same theme the surrounding chapters have developed at length. Surah ar-Ra'd stated that speaking secretly or openly is the same to Him, and that hiding in the night is no different from moving in daylight.\n\nPlacing it inside a personal prayer gives the principle a different texture. It is not only a warning to those who plot in secret; it is a comfort to someone praying about matters too complicated to explain.",
  "39": "\"\"All praise is for Allah who has blessed me with Ishmael and Isaac in my old age.\"\" interrupts the sequence of requests with an expression of thanks, and the placement teaches something about how to pray.\n\nAbraham has been asking for a great deal. In the middle of it, he stops to name a gift already received.\n\nThe gift is enormous, and the phrase about old age is what makes it so. He and Sarah had grown old without children, and the announcement of Isaac's coming birth was met with astonishment by both of them, as Surah Hud records in detail. Ishmael had been born earlier to Hagar, and the Quran describes Abraham receiving that news as glad tidings of a forbearing son.\n\nTwo sons in old age, both of whom became prophets, and from whom two great prophetic lines descended. Through Isaac came Jacob, Joseph, Moses, David, Solomon, and Jesus. Through Ishmael came the Prophet Muhammad.\n\nNaming both sons together is significant. The Quran consistently honours both lines, and Muslims are instructed to make no distinction between the prophets. Abraham does not rank his children.\n\n\"My Lord is indeed the Hearer of ˹all˺ prayers.\" is his conclusion, and it is drawn from experience rather than from theory.\n\nHe is not stating a doctrine he was taught. He asked for a child when he was old and childless, and he was given two. That is the evidence behind the statement.\n\nThere is a practical model in this for anyone who prays. Gratitude for what has already been answered strengthens confidence in what is still being asked, and it keeps supplication from becoming a list of complaints.\n\nThe Arabic word for hearing here indicates responsive hearing, the kind that leads to an answer. He is not merely audible to Allah; he has been answered before.",
  "40": "\"\"My Lord! Make me and those ˹believers˺ of my descendants keep up prayer.\"\" returns to supplication after the interlude of thanks, and the request is for something Abraham had already been doing his whole life.\n\nHe asks to be made someone who establishes prayer. A man in old age, who had left his family in a barren valley specifically so that prayer would be established there, prays to be kept in that state.\n\nThis reflects a consistent attitude among the prophets. Past performance is never treated as a guarantee of future steadfastness, and continuation is asked for rather than assumed. The Prophet Muhammad frequently prayed to the One who turns hearts to keep his heart firm upon the religion.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates being one who establishes prayer, making it upright and regular. Abraham is asking to remain that kind of person.\n\n\"And those ˹believers˺ of my descendants\" extends the request across generations, which is where a parent's real concern lies. A person can influence his own conduct; he cannot control what his grandchildren will do long after he is gone.\n\nThe wording includes a qualification that commentators have noted. He asks for his descendants in a way that does not presume all of them will qualify, having been told earlier, as Surah al-Baqarah records, that Allah's covenant does not extend to wrongdoers among them.\n\n\"Our Lord! Accept my prayers.\" is the closing request, and it is the most humble part of the whole supplication.\n\nHaving asked for a great deal, he now asks that the asking itself be accepted. This assumes nothing. A prayer offered is not automatically a prayer received, and Abraham does not treat his own standing as sufficient.\n\nThe same attitude appears in the account of building the Kaaba, where he and Ishmael raise the foundations and ask their Lord to accept it from them. Work is done, and acceptance is requested rather than expected.",
  "41": "\"\"Our Lord! Forgive me, my parents, and the believers on the Day when the judgment will come to pass.\"\" closes Abraham's supplication, and its final request is the one that matters most.\n\nAfter security for a city, protection from idols, provision in a barren valley, and steadfastness in prayer, the last thing he asks for is forgiveness.\n\nIncluding his parents raises a question that deserves an honest answer, since his father was an idol worshipper who threatened to stone him. The Quran addresses this directly in Surah at-Tawbah, explaining that Abraham's asking forgiveness for his father was because of a promise he had made to him, and that when it became clear to him that his father was an enemy to Allah, he disowned him. Praying for a parent who has died in disbelief is not permitted, and this verse records a stage before that clarity came, alongside the honouring of parents that Islam requires in general.\n\n\"And the believers\" widens the prayer beyond his own family to everyone who believes, in every generation.\n\nThat inclusion reaches the person reading this now. Abraham, thousands of years ago, asked for the forgiveness of believers who had not yet been born, and anyone holding to faith today falls within his supplication.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad encouraged the same practice, and there is a hadith recorded by at-Tabarani indicating that whoever asks forgiveness for the believing men and women receives a good deed for each of them. Praying for people one has never met is treated in Islam as a genuine act of worship rather than a sentiment.\n\n\"On the Day when the judgment will come to pass\" specifies when the forgiveness is needed. Not for social comfort or peace of mind now, but for the moment of reckoning.\n\nAbraham's whole prayer, read from beginning to end, moves from a city to a family to a practice and finally to that Day. The direction of travel is instructive.",
  "42": "\"Do not think ˹O Prophet˺ that Allah is unaware of what the wrongdoers do.\" addresses a thought that occurs to anyone watching injustice continue without consequence.\n\nThe wording is directed to the Prophet, as similar warnings in these chapters are, while the real audience is everyone who has ever wondered why the wrongdoer prospers.\n\nThe temptation being addressed is not disbelief but a kind of quiet doubt. A person sees oppression, fraud, and cruelty going unpunished for years, and something in him begins to suspect that nobody is keeping account.\n\nThe Arabic denies any heedlessness on Allah's part. Nothing is missed, forgotten, or overlooked, which the surrounding chapters have established repeatedly.\n\n\"He only delays them until a Day when ˹their˺ eyes will stare in horror\" gives the explanation. The delay that looks like absence is postponement to a fixed moment.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's most consistent teachings, and it appears throughout these chapters. Surah Hud described a grip that is painful and severe when it finally comes, and the Prophet commented on it by saying that Allah gives respite to the wrongdoer until, when He seizes him, He does not let him escape.\n\nThe image chosen for that Day is the eyes. Not the punishment itself, not the reckoning, but the appearance of a face at the moment of realisation.\n\nThe Arabic indicates eyes fixed open, staring without movement, in the way that extreme shock freezes a person's expression. Anyone who has seen someone receive devastating news recognises this.\n\nThere is something deliberate in the choice. These were people whose eyes had been described throughout the Quran as blind to the signs. Now the same eyes are wide open and unable to close, seeing exactly what they refused to see.\n\nThe description continues into the next verse, and it becomes stranger and more disturbing.",
  "43": "\"Rushing forth, heads raised, never blinking, hearts void.\" completes the picture of that Day in four short phrases, and each one adds a detail to a single unforgettable image.\n\nThey are rushing, hurrying forward. The Arabic indicates moving quickly with the neck outstretched, and it suggests being driven rather than choosing to run. These are people who spent their lives refusing to move toward the truth, now moving without any ability to stop.\n\nTheir heads are raised, fixed upward. The Arabic describes the head tilted back so that the gaze cannot be lowered. Commentators have noted the reversal in this. In life these were the arrogant, who looked down on others and would not lower their heads before Allah. Now the head is locked in a position they cannot change.\n\nThey never blink. Their eyes do not return to them, which is a striking way of describing a stare so fixed that the eyelid does not fall. Blinking is involuntary and constant, and its absence indicates a state of shock beyond anything ordinary experience provides.\n\nTheir hearts are void. The Arabic indicates emptiness, hollowness, and commentators explained it in several ways: hearts emptied of understanding, of any thought, of everything except terror. Some described it as the heart rising up so that nothing remains in its place.\n\nRead together, the four details describe a person who has lost every form of composure at once: motion he does not control, a posture he cannot alter, eyes he cannot close, and a heart with nothing left in it.\n\nThe Quran gives this description not to frighten for its own sake but to make something imaginable that people routinely dismiss because they cannot picture it.\n\nSet against the rest of revelation, the hollow heart is the inverse of a picture drawn elsewhere. Surah ar-Ra'd describes believers as those whose 'hearts find comfort in the remembrance of Allah.' What a heart carries through life either steadies it on that Day or leaves it standing empty, and the Quran holds both portraits side by side.\n\nWhat follows immediately is the instruction to warn, which is the whole purpose of such a passage.",
  "44": "\"And warn the people of the Day when the punishment will overtake ˹the wicked among˺ them\" gives the instruction that follows the description just delivered.\n\nThe Prophet is told to warn, and the previous verses supplied the material. A warning requires a picture, which is why the Quran describes such scenes in detail.\n\n\"And the wrongdoers will cry, \"Our Lord! Delay us for a little while.\"\" records what they will ask for, and it is the most human request in the passage.\n\nThey want time. Not wealth, not status, not any of the things they spent their lives pursuing. A short extension.\n\n\"We will respond to Your call and follow the messengers!\" is what they promise to do with it, and the promise itself is an admission. They are conceding that the call was genuine and that the messengers were telling the truth. Everything they denied is acknowledged in a single sentence, at the moment when acknowledgement is worthless.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the size of the request. They ask for a little while, as though a short period would be enough to change everything. Yet they had already been given decades, and the Quran elsewhere points out that people were given enough life for anyone who wished to take heed to do so.\n\n\"˹It will be said,˺ \"Did you not swear before that you would never be removed ˹to the next life˺?\"\" is the reply, and it quotes them back to themselves.\n\nThey had sworn oaths denying resurrection. The Quran records such oaths in several places, describing people who swore by Allah with their strongest oaths that He would not raise the dead.\n\nThe answer does not argue with their request. It simply reminds them of their own confident position, which is a more complete refutation than any argument could be.\n\nThe exchange ends there, and the following verses turn to the evidence they had walked past for years.",
  "45": "\"You passed by the ruins of those ˹destroyed peoples˺ who had wronged themselves.\" continues the address to those asking for more time, and it points to evidence they had already been given.\n\nThe Arabic indicates dwelling in or passing through the places where earlier peoples had lived. The Meccans travelled these routes regularly. The remains of Thamud lay along the road north toward Syria, and the region associated with Lot's cities lay in the same direction.\n\nThese were not distant legends. They were sites people camped near on trade journeys, and the Prophet later instructed his companions not to enter such dwellings except while weeping, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nThe description of those peoples is precise: they wronged themselves. This has been the chapter's consistent verdict, and it removes any suggestion that they were victims of arbitrary destruction.\n\n\"It was made clear to you how We dealt with them\" states that the meaning of those ruins had been explained. The Quran had recounted the accounts of Noah, Ad, Thamud, Lot, and Midian at length in the chapters just before this one. Nobody could claim the evidence was ambiguous or that no one had interpreted it for them.\n\n\"And We gave you ˹many˺ examples.\" adds that comparisons and parables had been supplied as well. This same chapter had offered the good tree with firm roots, the evil tree with none, and the deeds like ashes scattered by a storm.\n\nThe cumulative point is difficult to escape. They had physical evidence in the landscape, historical accounts explaining it, and comparisons designed to make the meaning unmistakable.\n\nAsking for more time, in that context, is asking for something that had already been given repeatedly. The problem was never the amount of time or the quantity of evidence, which is exactly what the preceding chapters said about people who would not believe even if every sign came to them.",
  "46": "\"They devised every plot, which was fully known to Allah\" describes the effort these people put into opposing the message.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that they schemed their scheme, and the construction suggests they used everything available to them. In Mecca this included ridicule, boycott, torture of the weak, attempts to bribe the Prophet, negotiations to make him compromise, and eventually a plan to kill him in his bed, which the Quran records in Surah al-Anfal.\n\nThe comment that their plotting was fully known to Allah removes the essential ingredient of any scheme. Secrecy is what makes plotting work, and it did not exist.\n\n\"But their plotting was not enough to ˹even˺ overpower mountains ˹let alone Allah˺.\" is the assessment, and the comparison is deliberately chosen.\n\nCommentators have read the Arabic in more than one way. On the reading reflected here, their schemes could not move mountains, meaning they were far too weak to shift even a physical feature of the earth, let alone to frustrate the plan of the One who made it.\n\nSome scholars offered a different reading, in which their plotting was so severe that mountains could have been moved by it, which serves as an expression of its magnitude while still leaving it futile against Allah.\n\nEither way the conclusion is the same, and the choice of mountains as the measure is effective. Mountains are the standard image of immovability. A scheme that cannot budge a hill is being compared with a target that created the hill.\n\nThere is something in this for anyone facing organised opposition. The plotting is real, and it causes genuine harm. The early Muslims suffered from every one of the plans listed above. What the verse denies is not the existence of the harm but the ultimate effectiveness of the plan.\n\nSurah ar-Ra'd said the same in different words, noting that those before them plotted and that the ultimate plan belongs to Allah.",
  "47": "\"So do not think ˹O Prophet˺ that Allah will fail to keep His promise to His messengers.\" repeats the form used earlier in this chapter, where the Prophet was told not to imagine that Allah is unaware of what wrongdoers do.\n\nBoth warnings address thoughts that arise naturally under prolonged pressure. The first concerns whether anyone is watching; this one concerns whether the promised outcome will actually arrive.\n\nThe promise to the messengers has been stated repeatedly in these chapters: rescue for the believers, and the destruction of those who wronged themselves. Every account in Surah Hud ended with exactly that pattern.\n\nFor a man in Mecca who had watched his companions tortured and had been driven out of Ta'if, the promise had not yet materialised. The verse addresses that gap directly.\n\nThe Arabic indicates breaking a promise, failing to fulfil what was pledged. Its denial regarding Allah is absolute, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated the same, that He never fails in His promise.\n\nUnderstanding why this is certain requires noticing why human promises fail. A person breaks his word for three reasons: he forgets, he becomes unable, or he changes his mind. None of these can apply to the One described in this chapter as knowing what is concealed and revealed, and as able to remove an entire creation and replace it without difficulty.\n\n\"Allah is indeed Almighty, capable of punishment.\" gives the two attributes that guarantee it.\n\nThe Arabic \"Aziz\" indicates might that cannot be overcome. The second phrase indicates the possessor of retribution, the One who exacts what is due.\n\nBoth are necessary for a promise of this kind. Might without the will to act would leave the wrongdoers untouched, and the will to act without power would be an empty threat.\n\nPlaced at the end of a passage about plotters whose schemes could not move a mountain, the two names identify who they were actually plotting against.",
  "48": "\"˹Watch for˺ the Day ˹when˺ the earth will be changed into a different earth and the heavens as well\" describes something that goes beyond anything the chapter has yet mentioned.\n\nPrevious verses described what happens to people on that Day. This one describes what happens to the setting itself.\n\nThe Arabic indicates substitution, one thing exchanged for another. Commentators discussed whether this means an entirely new creation or a transformation of the existing one, and reports from the Companions describe the earth becoming a level, white plain with no landmark on it, an expanse where nothing familiar remains.\n\nWhatever the precise nature of the change, the effect is the removal of every reference point. Mountains that stood for millions of years, cities, coastlines, and borders all disappear. Everything human beings used to orient themselves is gone.\n\nThere is something in this that addresses a deep human assumption. People treat the physical world as the permanent backdrop against which temporary lives are lived. The verse reverses that. The people continue; the backdrop does not.\n\n\"And all will appear before Allah\" describes what happens on that new ground. The Arabic indicates emerging into full view, coming out into the open where nothing is hidden.\n\nThe same word was used earlier in this chapter for the moment when followers and leaders appeared before Him and began blaming each other. Everything concealed becomes visible, which is a consistent theme in the Quran's descriptions of that Day.\n\n\"The One, the Supreme.\" closes the verse with two names that answer the whole scene.\n\n\"Al-Wahid\" indicates the One, unique, without partner. \"Al-Qahhar\" indicates the Irresistible, who overpowers everything and is overpowered by nothing.\n\nJoseph used exactly this pair when speaking to two prisoners about which was better: many scattered lords or Allah, the One, the Supreme. On the Day this verse describes, the answer will require no argument.",
  "49": "\"On that Day you will see the wicked bound together in chains\" begins the final image of the chapter, and it shows the condition of those who had spent their lives free to do as they wished.\n\nThe Arabic word for those described here indicates criminals, people who deliberately cut themselves off from what is right. The same term appeared in Surah Yunus, where such people were asked whether they realised what they were demanding to be hastened.\n\nThey are bound together, joined in shackles. Commentators explained that those who cooperated in wrongdoing will be linked, and some described each person being coupled with the devil who accompanied him, which the Quran mentions elsewhere.\n\nBeing chained together carries a particular meaning after what this chapter has already described. The exchange between the arrogant leaders and their followers showed each side trying to escape the other. The leaders disowned their followers, and Satan disowned everyone. Now they are physically joined to the very company they were trying to get away from.\n\nThere is an appropriateness in this that the verse does not need to explain. In life these were people bound together by shared interest, mutual flattery, and cooperation in harm. The bonds they formed voluntarily become bonds they cannot break.\n\nThe image also reverses their worldly condition precisely. These were the ones with freedom of movement, with power to come and go, to impose their will and to walk away from consequences. The chapter described their leaders as arrogant, exalting themselves above others.\n\nChains remove all of it at once.\n\nThe Arabic word for these fetters indicates heavy iron restraints of the kind used on prisoners, and it is the same image used in Surah ar-Ra'd, where those who denied resurrection were described as having shackles around their necks.\n\nThe description continues into the final verse, adding two further details.",
  "50": "\"With garments of tar, and their faces covered with flames.\" closes the chapter with two final details, and both are chosen for reasons worth understanding.\n\nThe Arabic word for tar indicates a thick, black, flammable substance, used in that world for coating the hulls of ships and for treating the skin of camels against disease. It is sticky, foul-smelling, and burns readily.\n\nDescribing it as a garment is deliberate. Clothing is what a person puts on to be seen in, and much human effort goes into what one wears and the impression it creates. These were people of standing, and their appearance was part of their authority.\n\nWhat they wear now covers them completely, clings to the skin, and feeds the fire rather than protecting against it.\n\n\"And their faces covered with flames\" is the second detail, and it is the more disturbing of the two.\n\nThe face is what a person shows to the world. It carries identity, dignity, and expression, and the Quran describes faces on that Day repeatedly: some bright and laughing, others dusty and covered in darkness.\n\nHere the face is enveloped in fire. Whatever a person was recognised by is gone.\n\nThere is a connection worth noticing between this and the arrogance described throughout the chapter. These were the ones who raised their heads, looked down on the weak, and treated their standing as the measure of everything. The final image removes the face entirely.\n\nThe chapter ends here, and the placement of this verse is not accidental. It began by describing a Book sent to bring people out of darkness into light, and it closes with the condition of those who chose to remain where they were.\n\nBetween the two lie the tree with firm roots, the prayer of Abraham, the countless blessings that cannot be numbered, and the repeated instruction to warn while there is still time.",
  "51": "\"As such, Allah will reward every soul for what it has committed.\" states the principle behind everything the chapter has just described.\n\nThe opening phrase points backward. The chains, the garments of tar, the faces covered in flames: all of it is recompense rather than arbitrary cruelty. The Arabic word for reward here covers repayment in both directions, and it indicates giving a person exactly what corresponds to his deed.\n\n\"Every soul\" leaves nobody out. The arrogant leaders and the followers who blamed them, described earlier in the chapter, are covered by the same rule. So are the believers whose prayers and quiet charity were mentioned a few verses back.\n\nThe wording is worth noticing: what it has committed, meaning what the soul itself earned. This is the chapter's consistent position, and it closes off every excuse the passage has already recorded. The followers could not transfer their guilt to their leaders. Satan told his followers plainly not to blame him but to blame themselves. Each account is individual.\n\n\"Surely Allah is swift in reckoning.\" adds the assurance that this vast accounting will not be slow.\n\nThe phrase has appeared in the preceding chapters, and it carries two meanings that both apply. He does not need time to examine evidence or weigh testimony, since the knowledge is already complete. And what feels like a long delay to human beings is nothing at all against the scale involved.\n\nThere is something in this for anyone troubled by the sheer number of people who have ever lived. A human court struggles with a single complicated case. Nothing here is complicated for the One who already knows every deed of every person, including what was concealed.\n\nPlaced at the end of the descriptions of punishment, the verse functions as a defence of divine justice. Nobody receives more than he earned, nobody receives less, and nobody waits indefinitely for the matter to be settled.",
  "52": "\"This ˹Quran˺ is a ˹sufficient˺ message for humanity\" closes the chapter by describing what the whole Book is meant to be.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a communication delivered, something conveyed so that it reaches its intended recipients. The same root produced the word used earlier for the Prophet's duty, which was only to deliver.\n\nDescribing it as a message for humanity widens the address beyond the Meccans who had been arguing throughout the chapter. This is not tribal instruction or a national religion, which matters in a Book that opened by describing revelation as a means of bringing people out of darkness into light.\n\n\"So that they may take it as a warning\" gives the first purpose. The chapter has supplied plenty of material for that: the ruins passed on the trade routes, the deeds scattered like ashes, the eyes that will not blink.\n\n\"And know that there is only One God\" gives the second, and it is the central claim of everything the Quran teaches. Notice the verb: know, not merely believe or accept. The chapter has argued for this by pointing at rain, ships, the sun and moon, and blessings too numerous to count, and each argument was an appeal to something a person can examine.\n\n\"And so that people of reason may be mindful.\" names who actually benefits, and it closes with the same appeal to intelligence that runs through these chapters.\n\nThe Arabic term indicates those possessing sound understanding, people who use the faculty they were given rather than following inherited habit.\n\nThere is a pattern worth noticing in how this chapter ends. It began with a Book sent to lead people out of darkness, and it finishes by describing that Book as a warning, a source of knowledge, and a reminder for those who think.\n\nBetween the two lie Abraham's prayer in a barren valley and the good tree whose roots hold firm."
};
